    <statement><p>Suppose <m>f(x) = x^{3}\mathopen{}\left(7-2x^{9}\right)</m>.</p>
<p>(a) The roots of <m>f(x)</m> are 
<m>x =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>(If a root is repeated, enter it only once.)</p>
<p>(b) As <m>x \to \infty</m>, 
<m>f(x) \to</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>(b) As <m>x \to -\infty</m>, 
<m>f(x) \to</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0, 1.14935</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-\infty </m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\infty </m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><alert>  SOLUTION </alert></p>
<p>(a) To find the roots, we solve <m>x^{3}\mathopen{}\left(7-2x^{9}\right) = 0</m> for <m>x</m>.  
Either <m>x^{3} = 0</m>, in which case <m>x = 0</m>, or
<m>7 - 2 x^{9}  = 0</m>, in which case 
<m>\displaystyle x = \sqrt[9]{\frac{7}{2}}</m>.</p>
<p>(b) Since the leading term is <m>-2 x^{12}</m> and
<m>-2 x^{12} \to -\infty</m> as <m>x \to \infty</m>, 
we have <m>x^{3}\mathopen{}\left(7-2x^{9}\right) \to -\infty</m> as <m>x \to \infty</m>.</p>
<p>(c) Since the leading term is <m>-2 x^{12}</m> and
<m>-2 x^{12} \to \infty</m> as <m>x \to -\infty</m>, 
we have <m>x^{3}\mathopen{}\left(7-2x^{9}\right) \to \infty</m> as <m>x \to -\infty</m>.</p>
</solution>
